==Dutch==

===Etymology===
{{dercat|nl|odt|frk|gmw-pro|gem-pro|ine-pro|inh=4}}
From {{inh|nl|dum|sullen|solen, suelen, sollen, sullen}}, from {{inh|nl|odt|sulan}}, from {{inh|nl|gmw-pro|*skulan}}, from {{inh|nl|gem-pro|*skulaną}}. Compare {{cog|de|sollen}}, {{cog|en|shall}}, {{cog|da|skulle}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|nl|/ˈzʏ.lə(n)/}}
* {{audio|nl|Nl-zullen.ogg}}
* {{hyphenation|nl|zul|len}}
* {{rhymes|nl|ʏlən|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{nl-verb}}

# {{senseid|nl|future}} {{lb|nl|auxiliary|present tense}} {{ng|used to form the [[future tense]] of a verb, together with an [[infinitive]]}}; [[will]], [[going to]]
#: {{ux|nl|Ik '''zal''' zingen.|I '''shall''' sing.}}
#: {{ux|nl|Het '''zal''' toch niet werken.|It '''will''' not work anyway.}}
#: {{ux|nl|Dat '''zal''' niet gebeuren.|That '''will''' not happen.}}
# {{senseid|nl|conditional}} {{lb|nl|auxiliary|past tense}} {{ng|used to form the [[conditional mood]] of a verb, together with an infinitive}}; [[would]]
#: {{ux|nl|Het werk '''zou''' nu af zijn geweest als jij je niet had verslapen.|The work '''would''' have been finished now if you hadn't overslept.}}
#: {{ux|nl|Dat '''zou''' ik niet doen als ik jou was.|I '''would''' not do that if I were you.}}
# {{senseid|nl|modal}} {{lb|nl|modal auxiliary}} {{ng|used to indicate [[intent]] or [[disposition]] to perform an action in the [[future]]}}: [[will]], [[shall]], be [[going to]], [[may]], [[might]]
#: {{ux|nl|Het '''zou''' vandaag regenen.|It '''was going to''' rain today.}}
#: {{ux|nl|'''Zal''' ik eerst gaan?|'''Shall''' I go first?}}

====Usage notes====
* In the second-person singular, the forms {{m|nl||je&#8201;/&#8201;u zult}} and {{m|nl||je&#8201;/&#8201;u zal}} are both entirely correct, but the latter is considered more informal in the Netherlands; especially {{m|nl||u zal}} is rarely seen in writing. In Belgium both sets of forms are used interchangeably.<ref>[https://www.onzetaal.nl/taalloket/je-zult-je-zal Wat is goed: ‘Je zult wel moe zijn’ of ‘Je zal wel moe zijn’?] at OnzeTaal.nl</ref><ref>[https://taaladvies.net/u-wil-zal-kan-of-wilt-zult-kunt/ U wil, zal, kan / wilt, zult, kunt] at Taaladvies.net</ref><ref>[https://e-ans.ivdnt.org/topics/pid/ans02030606lingtopic 2.3.6.6 Zullen] in Algemene Nederlandse Spraakkunst</ref> Historically, ''u'' (''U. E.'' in obsolete usage) was a third-person pronoun and very often took ''zal'' (or ''sal''), although second-person forms of ''zullen'' are often attested early as well.
* Like most Germanic languages, Dutch prefers to use the present tense when the context already expresses the future: {{m|nl||Volgende week koop ik een nieuwe auto.|t=Next week I [will] buy a new car.}} The future tense is used chiefly when needed for comprehension. Using it in sentences such as the aforementioned is grammatical, but may sound clumsy especially when repeated in consecutive sentences.

